WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.520
Whether they were burnt out after multiple seasons&nbsp;
or killed off to make room for another actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.434
these sci-fi stars left their most&nbsp;
popular shows for all kinds of reasons.

00:00:10.434 --> 00:00:15.600
As FBI agents Fox Mulder and Dana Scully,&nbsp;
played by David Duchovny and Gillian Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.540
tackled mysterious cases involving supernatural&nbsp;
phenomena on "The X-Files." Mulder always assumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.740
a paranormal link, while Scully takes&nbsp;
a more skeptical approach. But after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.060
Duchovny chose to leave the series at the&nbsp;
end of season seven, the show just wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.380
the same. In a statement released after the&nbsp;
news of his departure broke, Duchovny said,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.580
"As much as I love the show, I think for&nbsp;
me this will be the end. I always thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.760
five years was enough. Seven&nbsp;
years is definitely enough."

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.320
Duchovny's contract was officially up at the&nbsp;
end of the seventh season — and rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.640
renewing it, he de cided to leave. Another&nbsp;
factor also prompted him to say goodbye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.160
however. Duchovny was also stuck in a legal battle&nbsp;
with Fox over syndication revenue, as he felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.080
Fox was taking in greater profits from reruns&nbsp;
while reducing his own earnings from royalties.

00:01:01.272 --> 00:01:02.892
"Sounds like a motive."

00:01:02.892 --> 00:01:08.280
The original series continued on for two more&nbsp;
seasons, with Duchovny making only occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.780
appearances. However, he did return to his&nbsp;
leading role in the tenth and eleventh seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.555
which aired in 2016 and 2018, well&nbsp;
after the initial run had wrapped up.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.380
In the mid-2000s, there might not have been&nbsp;
any show hotter than "Lost," the polarizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.400
sci-fi survivalist thriller. While the&nbsp;
show saw plenty of characters come and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.600
go over the course of its lengthy run,&nbsp;
none were as controversial as Mr. Eko,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.120
the drug dealer and thug-turned-priest played&nbsp;
by Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje. Introduced at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.480
the start of the second season, he was&nbsp;
killed off in that same year's finale.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.500
Though many fans have argued about how little&nbsp;
of "Lost" was well-planned, there seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.360
be other reasons for Akinnuoye-Agbaje's&nbsp;
departure, as he unexpectedly asked out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.440
the series before the season had wrapped&nbsp;
up. According to producer Carlton Cuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.600
Akinnuoye-Agbaje never felt comfortable in Hawaii&nbsp;
— where he had moved to shoot the series — a fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.280
not helped by his arrest by local police&nbsp;
landed him in jail. Cuse told USA Today,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.120
"Hawaii was not his bag. Real&nbsp;
life intervened and forced us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.820
end the arc of that character much&nbsp;
sooner than we would have liked."

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.100
In 2010, Akinnuoye-Agbaje was said to have been&nbsp;
offered a cameo in the series' epic finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.820
where he would have returned as the&nbsp;
faithful Mr. Eko. But according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.600
E! Online, he turned it down —&nbsp;
reportedly due to a pay dispute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.007
as the actor was said to have been&nbsp;
looking for far more than he was offered.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.180
"I am not sorry for this. I am proud of this."

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.480
The first "Star Wars" series on Disney+, "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian," stars Pedro Pascal behind that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.680
iconic mask. There's plenty of familiar&nbsp;
faces in the supporting cast as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.920
including former MMA fighter Gina Carano. But not&nbsp;
long after the show's second season concluded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.940
it was announced that Carano was&nbsp;
out, and it was no secret as to why.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.820
According to THR, rumors that Disney was looking&nbsp;
to axe the actress began circulating after she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.740
made a series of controversial social media&nbsp;
posts regarding multiple polarizing topics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:07.320
From comments on the Covid vaccines to voter fraud&nbsp;
in the 2020 United States Presidential election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:13.380
Carano did not keep her thoughts to herself. The&nbsp;
final straw was her anti-Semitic post in which she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.180
compared the experience of conservatives to the&nbsp;
suffering of Jewish people during the Holocaust.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.840
In response to growing calls for her&nbsp;
dismissal among the public — led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.520
the hashtag #FireGinaCarano — Disney was&nbsp;
forced to issue a statement. The company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.420
said that she wasn't under contract, that&nbsp;
she wouldn't be continuing in the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.900
and that they condemn her online&nbsp;
comments. The release read,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.800
"Her social media posts denigrating&nbsp;
people based on their cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.555
and religious identities are&nbsp;
abhorrent and unacceptable."

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.140
Willa Holland was a child actress who grew up&nbsp;
amid the hustle and bustle of Hollywood thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.720
to her family roots, since her ex-stepfather is&nbsp;
"Scarface" director Brian De Palma. In the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.920
she starred in two of the hottest teen&nbsp;
dramas, "The O.C." and "Gossip Girl,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.380
before graduating to superheroics in The CW's&nbsp;
"Arrow." There she starred as Oliver Queen's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.120
upstart younger sister Thea, who eventually&nbsp;
trains to become a vigilante herself. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.620
ultimately takes on the super-identity of&nbsp;
Speedy and becomes Green Arrow's partner.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.640
Starring for six seasons in the hit&nbsp;
series, Holland was a stalwart of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.560
main cast and an integral part of Arrow's&nbsp;
universe. But in the middle of Season 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.420
audiences were left stunned when Speedy set&nbsp;
off on her own mission with boyfriend Roy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.460
Harper — the hero known as Arsenal. This wasn't&nbsp;
just a creative way for the writers to shake up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.080
the status quo in the lead-up to the final two&nbsp;
seasons, but an exit orchestrated by Holland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.940
herself. Producer and series co-creator&nbsp;
Marc Guggenheim told Entertainment Weekly,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:43.440
"Season 6 is the end of her contract.&nbsp;
Going into season 6, with all of us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.360
knowing it was the end of her contract,&nbsp;
Willa expressed the desire to move on."

00:04:47.662 --> 00:04:52.622
"Find the best version of myself, whoever she is."

00:04:52.622 --> 00:04:55.260
The decision was made to&nbsp;
not kill off the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.520
though, as producers had a fondness for&nbsp;
both Speedy and Holland. Sure enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.371
Holland returned for a handful of guest&nbsp;
appearances over the final seasons.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.660
For three seasons, Matt Smith took on&nbsp;
the role of the Doctor in Doctor Who.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:11.460
Head writer and executive producer&nbsp;
Steven Moffat was very impressed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.620
Smith from the beginning — after seeing him act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.060
he knew that Smith was the perfect choice&nbsp;
to play the eleventh Doctor. But ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.260
Smith became too burned out by the demands&nbsp;
of the role. He told The Hollywood Reporter —

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:27.300
"I'd always talked about leaving after the 50th&nbsp;
anniversary. It's a very intense process to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.920
the Doctor. The line-learning is really hard, and&nbsp;
you have to live away from home for nine or 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.780
months a year. I love the show, and it wasn't an&nbsp;
easy choice to come to, but it's the right time."

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.200
"So, what now, doctor?"
"Well. Time to get cracking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.820
doctor."
"Mhmm."

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.380
However, it seems that Smith now has mixed&nbsp;
feelings about his eventual decision to leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.480
as he has stated that he wished he could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.360
spent more time working with his&nbsp;
talented co-star Jenna Coleman.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:58.560
Debuting in 1993, Terry Farrell portrayed Jadzia&nbsp;
Dax in "Star Trek: Deep Space Nine," before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.420
character was killed off in the Season 6 finale to&nbsp;
the dismay of many. She'd been a stalwart series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.100
regular, was married to a fellow main character,&nbsp;
and was best friend to the show's captain.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.820
While fans may have been blindsided,&nbsp;
Farrell has been more than open about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.260
her exit from the series over the&nbsp;
years. In a 2011 chat with TrekMovie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.580
Farrell said she was feeling some burnout&nbsp;
after a six-year run. She explained,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.640
"I was just really tired. I was tired of waking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.760
at four in the morning. I was&nbsp;
tired of all the minutiae."

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.140
At the same time, her contract was up, and&nbsp;
she was receiving interest elsewhere around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.910
Hollywood. She decided to renegotiate for&nbsp;
less screen time: as she told Trek Today —

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.660
"I did suggest in the wake of that I could&nbsp;
be recurring [...] not be in every episode."

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:43.620
Instead, her Trill symbiont got a&nbsp;
new body in the form of Nicole De&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.300
Boer. In the 2018 documentary, "What We&nbsp;
Left Behind," Farrell was more candid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.500
describing how she had felt&nbsp;
disrespected by producers. She said,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.580
"The last season when I was told by one of the&nbsp;
producers that I was really lucky to be there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.120
and I really should sign the next contract&nbsp;
because if I weren't there on the set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.940
I'd be at K-Mart. That was the last straw for me."

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:08.435
Farrell eventually landed a lead role&nbsp;
in the NBC series "Becker" in 1998.

00:07:08.435 --> 00:07:13.020
In the late '90s, actor Jerry O'Connell&nbsp;
starred in "Sliders," a cult favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.620
that's better than you probably&nbsp;
remember. But after its third season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:22.620
the show moved from Fox to the Sci-Fi Channel —&nbsp;
and in a 1998 interview in the Chicago Tribune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.920
the actor expressed excitement&nbsp;
about the new direction, saying,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.360
"It's great to be on the Sci-Fi Channel because&nbsp;
we can cater to a more intelligent audience."

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.940
But as O'Connell recalled in a 2016 interview&nbsp;
with Yahoo, things didn't turn out the way he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.140
hoped. Instead of the show using effects&nbsp;
to explore parallel worlds in unique ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:45.120
O'Connell thought "Sliders" had become too&nbsp;
focused on action and fighting. His displeasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.860
with the show's altered tone coincided with the&nbsp;
expiration of his contract — and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.280
the actor felt that it was the&nbsp;
right time to step away, explaining,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.600
"I had learned all that I was going to learn&nbsp;
from the show after four seasons, and I decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.980
to leave when my contract was up. I was hoping&nbsp;
that they would go on with my brother, who joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:07.000
the cast in Season 4. They got pretty mad and&nbsp;
decided to reboot the whole thing and blow it up."

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.030
"I don't know if I'm ready for all that."

00:08:11.247 --> 00:08:16.020
Audiences seem to love live-action adaptations&nbsp;
of popular young adult sci-fi novel series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:20.580
and "The 100" is one of the biggest on the&nbsp;
small screen. Running for seven seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:25.860
it launched on The CW in 2014 and centered on&nbsp;
a group of people who come back to Earth almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.240
a hundred years after leaving in the wake&nbsp;
of nuclear armageddon. Despite its sprawling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.680
ensemble cast, only three actors starred&nbsp;
in all seven seasons. Ricky Whittle though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.160
could have made it four, were it not for his&nbsp;
leaving the show inexplicably after the during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.120
the third season. His character ended up&nbsp;
being killed off in an eye-popping twist.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.420
The real revelation came in&nbsp;
the aftermath of his departure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.500
when Whittle revealed behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
turmoil in an interview with Afterbuzz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.460
TV. He explained that it was his choice&nbsp;
to leave the show, and claimed that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:58.200
was serious beef between him and the series&nbsp;
showrunner Jason Rothenberg. Whittle said,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.460
"He was professionally bullying me, cutting&nbsp;
out all the storyline that I was supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:07.020
be doing. Cutting lines, cutting everything&nbsp;
out, trying to make my character and myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.560
as insignificant as possible to the point&nbsp;
where it was starting to get me down."

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.160
To get across just how bad things&nbsp;
were between them, Whittle added,

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.900
"If this was in the playground, it would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.100
got physical [...] but I'm an adult and&nbsp;
violence isn't going to solve anything."

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:24.420
After joining the long-running British sci-fi&nbsp;
smash "Doctor Who" in the titular role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.840
Christopher Eccleston found the famous&nbsp;
part to be more trouble than it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.280
worth. He ended up leaving the show after&nbsp;
only one season, leaving fans wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:36.540
why. Eccleston explained during a panel&nbsp;
discussion at New York Comic-Con in 2019,

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.220
"I left only because of those three individuals&nbsp;
and the way they were running the show. I loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.940
playing the character. I felt I was going&nbsp;
to play the Doctor my way and I wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.096
going to get involved with those politics&nbsp;
and that wasn't workable, so off I went."

00:09:49.096 --> 00:09:52.260
Eccleston eventually admitted that the&nbsp;
"three individuals" he had professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:57.480
conflicts with were the showrunner, producer,&nbsp;
and co-producer of the show. He also faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.740
other obstacles while playing the Doctor: he&nbsp;
struggled with an eating disorder while filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.700
and felt he was treated unfairly by&nbsp;
the media. Eventually, he reached a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.228
point where he felt that staying in the&nbsp;
role was no longer in his best interests.

00:10:09.487 --> 00:10:13.380
Before joining the cast of "Star Trek:&nbsp;
The Next Generation" as Wesley Crusher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.980
Wil Wheaton had already made a name for&nbsp;
himself with roles in films like "Stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.840
By Me." Playing Crusher represented a career&nbsp;
boost for Wheaton — but after a few seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.560
he decided to leave to pursue other&nbsp;
aspirations in Hollywood. Wheaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:30.180
explained during a panel discussion at the&nbsp;
Calgary Comic and Entertainment Expo in 2012,

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.960
"Initially I thought it was a really&nbsp;
smart business career move. In some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:37.320
ways it was and in more ways it&nbsp;
wasn't. What I wasn't prepared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:40.020
for was how much I was going to&nbsp;
miss the people on this stage."

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:42.180
He admitted that he didn't make much of an effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.360
to stay in touch with the rest of&nbsp;
the cast after making his exit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.920
and eventually, he felt embarrassed about&nbsp;
his decision to leave the show. He added,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.480
"I just felt really ashamed of myself. I&nbsp;
felt like I just couldn't go to the set — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.720
felt like I couldn't look them in the eye and&nbsp;
that I couldn't invite them to my wedding."

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:02.520
Fortunately, years later, he was able to&nbsp;
reconnect with his former co-stars — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.307
unsurprisingly, they had no hard feelings&nbsp;
towards him for leaving the show.

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:11.280
On the military science fiction series&nbsp;
"Stargate SG-1," Don S. Davis played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.240
George Hammond, who runs the military&nbsp;
base Stargate Command. Hammond is a stern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.540
straight-laced guy who genuinely cares&nbsp;
about the people under his watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.980
his characterization possibly inspired by&nbsp;
Davis' own experiences in the military.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:28.020
Davis eventually admitted that he had to leave&nbsp;
Stargate earlier than he had intended. Why did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.920
he have to quit? He was dealing with multiple&nbsp;
health problems that made it very difficult for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:36.000
him to continue acting. Davis stated that the&nbsp;
showrunners were always willing to work around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.800
his surgeries, doctor's appointments, and&nbsp;
other scheduling conflicts — but eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.420
he simply couldn't keep pushing ahead with work.&nbsp;
Davis explained in an interview with GateWorld,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.260
"'Stargate' stood by me. They made sure&nbsp;
that there was nothing that I was asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:53.040
to do that might put me in harm's way. But&nbsp;
there comes a time where you really can't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.640
if you're in my condition. I'm&nbsp;
a heart patient. I'm a diabetic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.940
I've got other minor problems, and&nbsp;
it's really time to slow down."

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.380
Sadly, Davis passed away after&nbsp;
suffering a heart attack in 2008.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.120
"Star Trek" is one of the most well-known&nbsp;
sci-fi franchises of all time, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.780
every actor involved is satisfied with&nbsp;
their character's arc. Denise Crosby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:16.020
who played Security Chief Tasha Yar&nbsp;
in "Star Trek: The Next Generation,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:19.680
didn't think that the writers gave her&nbsp;
enough interesting material to work with.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.820
Over time, Crosby became more and more&nbsp;
frustrated with the way Tasha Yar was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:28.140
portrayed, as she started to feel like more of&nbsp;
a prop than an actress. She has stated that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.980
out of all the characters she has played,&nbsp;
Tasha was the one she held nearest to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:35.280
heart — but eventually, she realized&nbsp;
that she would never be able to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:39.840
the character further. Crosby explained&nbsp;
in a 1996 interview with StarTrek.com,

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.440
"I wanted to leave as I was struggling&nbsp;
with not being able to do much with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:48.360
character. I had all these ideas and couldn't&nbsp;
do them. I was just stage dressing. I chose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:52.557
leave instead of just being satisfied with&nbsp;
that. I've never regretted my decision."

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.545
"I'm not supposed to be here, sir."

00:12:56.632 --> 00:13:00.780
The superhero sci-fi show "Misfits" was&nbsp;
an unexpected hit in the United Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:05.400
and helped to launch the careers of several&nbsp;
rising stars, including Robert Sheehan. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:10.020
"Misfits," Sheehan starred as Nathan Young, a&nbsp;
man who realizes that he is immortal when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:15.000
wakes up in his own coffin. But after an exciting&nbsp;
character arc, Sheehan decided to quit while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:20.160
was ahead and take his career in a new direction&nbsp;
after two seasons. Sheehan told Digital Spy,

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:25.080
"I was absolutely quite happy about when I went&nbsp;
off, it seemed like a no-brainer at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:28.440
because I was just a restless fella&nbsp;
who was trying to go off and do as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.860
much different stuff as possible. I'd&nbsp;
done two good summers with that show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.380
so [it] was wildly impatient to do other stuff."

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.940
It turned out to be a good career move, proving&nbsp;
that "Misfits" opened up doors for him. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:43.680
his departure, Sheehan landed a regular role&nbsp;
as Darren on "Love/Hate," and more recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:47.180
the role of fan-favorite Klaus&nbsp;
Hargreeves on "The Umbrella Academy."

00:13:47.351 --> 00:13:53.160
Corin Nemec ended up landing a part on "Stargate&nbsp;
SG-1" in an unexpected way. Michael Shanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.820
who had played Daniel Jackson, left the&nbsp;
show after season five, and the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:01.200
didn't want to leave a void in the SG-1 team.&nbsp;
Nemec happened to be auditioning for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:05.760
role when he bumped into the casting team for&nbsp;
"Stargate SG-1." They thought he seemed like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:10.140
the perfect fit to play Jonas Quinn, a new&nbsp;
character they would be adding to the show.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:14.880
Nemec loved working on "Stargate," and even got&nbsp;
to pitch ideas for different episodes. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.620
Shanks was able to come back for another season.&nbsp;
Nemec hoped to stay on board too — but the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.340
decided it was time for Jonas Quinn to go.&nbsp;
When asked why he was written off the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:27.180
Nemec admitted that he didn't know why the&nbsp;
writers couldn't have simply incorporated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.840
both his character and Michael&nbsp;
Shanks' into the storyline. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:35.175
he suspected that budget limitations may&nbsp;
have been a major factor in the decision.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:39.240
When "Star Trek: Voyager" first launched,&nbsp;
it featured several new alien characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:43.920
whose presence helped set the show apart from&nbsp;
previous spin-offs. This included the duo of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:48.240
quirky cook Neelix and the child-like Ocompan&nbsp;
Kes, the latter of whom was played by actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:53.520
Jennifer Lien. In Season 4, a new cast member&nbsp;
joined — the fan-favorite former Borg drone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:58.800
Seven of Nine, played by Jeri Ryan — and it's&nbsp;
no coincidence that her arrival coincided with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:03.420
Lien's departure. Series producer Jeri&nbsp;
Taylor said in a DVD special feature,

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:06.000
"The studio felt that we had too many characters."

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.580
Because Seven of Nine was&nbsp;
going to join the regular cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:12.900
someone had to go. Lien drew the short&nbsp;
straw, though it wasn't entirely a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:16.860
random choice: Brannon Braga told&nbsp;
an audience at the 2014 VegasCon,

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:20.820
"To my recollection it was a creative&nbsp;
decision, and it was a failure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.600
imagination on the writers' part. We were&nbsp;
running out of things to do with Kes."

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:30.120
Still, the series didn't say goodbye forever, as&nbsp;
Kes returned for the Season 6 episode "Fury." That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.260
episode helped wrap up the long-running story&nbsp;
of the character's abbreviated life cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:39.000
and gave her a more fitting send-off&nbsp;
than she'd seen in Season 4's "The Gift."

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:43.260
There are two generations of Richard Dean&nbsp;
Anderson fans. The first know him for his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:48.360
as the crafty do-gooder MacGyver, whose titular&nbsp;
series made him an '80s icon. But there's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:53.640
generation of fans who know him best for his role&nbsp;
as Jack O'Neill in "Stargate SG-1." Anderson had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:57.660
taken over the role from action icon Kurt&nbsp;
Russell, who'd starred in the film the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:02.460
had been adapted from — but he found a way to&nbsp;
make it his own. Unfortunately, following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:07.080
show's eighth season, Anderson departed, and&nbsp;
it was never the same. So why did he leave?

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:12.540
Apparently, it all came down to priorities.&nbsp;
In 1998, Anderson and his partner April A.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:17.700
Prose had a child, and by 2004, Anderson&nbsp;
was feeling the pull of family. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:23.280
Gateworld.com covered his appearance at Fan Expo&nbsp;
Canada, where they discussed his exit from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:27.840
show, citing his desire to spend more time with&nbsp;
his wife and daughter. More than a decade after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:31.500
leaving the series, though, Anderson said&nbsp;
he would have no problems with the notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:35.700
of returning to the role — with concrete&nbsp;
limitations. He told fans at the event,

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:40.560
"As far as punching the clock and going to work&nbsp;
I wouldn't mind doing a mini-series of some kind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.927
Something that had a clear beginning and end.&nbsp;
[...] I would do something along those lines."

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:51.000
In 2021, a new version of Superman hit the small&nbsp;
screen with "Superman &amp; Lois," an action-adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.620
series that sees the famous comic book couple&nbsp;
raising a pair of teenagers. Jordan Elsass and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.620 --> 00:17:00.600
Alex Garfin play Clark Kent's sons, Jonathan and&nbsp;
Jordan. Following the first two seasons, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.900
Elsass made a surprise announcement&nbsp;
that he would be leaving the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.100
and a report in Deadline mentioned sources&nbsp;
claiming that Elsass was dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:12.420
personal issues. The actor sat down with The&nbsp;
Wrap about a week later to clear the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.120
revealing a struggle with&nbsp;
his mental health. He said,

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:19.380
"It's sad, it's a real shame. I know&nbsp;
that I was pumped for Season 3, for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:24.120
but what are you going to do? Anyway, mental&nbsp;
health is definitely 100-percent priority.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:28.860
It's pinnacle. It's got to take precedence.&nbsp;
And mine has been [...] it's been rough."

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.740
Though Elsass didn't go any&nbsp;
further into his experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:35.880
it sounds like he may step away from&nbsp;
Hollywood altogether. He elaborated,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:40.617
"I need some time to myself. I'm still debating&nbsp;
whether I'm even going to act for a while."

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:46.200
Sadly, "Star Trek" is still losing stars to this&nbsp;
day, with "Star Trek: Discovery" abandoning actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:51.660
Shazad Latif after just two seasons. While his&nbsp;
co-stars Jason Isaacs and Michelle Yeoh were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:55.620
never intended to last very long, Latif's&nbsp;
situation was something a bit different.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:18:00.480
In the first season, Latif starred as Lieutenant&nbsp;
Ash Tyler, while his dual role as the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.340
Klingon Voq was kept a tightly guarded secret —&nbsp;
after all, it sat at the center of the season's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:10.740
major plot twist. But he returned in Season 2&nbsp;
as Tyler, who'd abandoned his Klingon identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:14.760
and began a romantic relationship with&nbsp;
series lead Michael Burnham. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:19.380
character said a surprising goodbye in the&nbsp;
Season 2 finale, leaving to head up the covert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:25.020
intelligence agency known as Section 31. This&nbsp;
isn't a case of Latif requesting off of the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:30.240
though — quite the opposite in fact. In a 2022&nbsp;
interview, the actor revealed just what happened.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:35.040
According to a report from TrekMovie, producers&nbsp;
had been planning a spin-off series focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:40.440
Section 31 to star Latif alongside superstar&nbsp;
Michelle Yeoh. But because of Yeoh's limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:44.760
availability, the series has yet to get&nbsp;
off the ground, leaving Latif in limbo.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.280
"You understand what I'm saying?"

00:18:48.905 --> 00:18:54.480
Aml Ameen played Capheus on the first season of&nbsp;
"Sense8," but fans were thus surprised when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:58.800
found out that the same character would be&nbsp;
played by actor Toby Onwumere in the second.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:03.120
It seems that Ameen's departure was a result&nbsp;
of some kind of behind-the-scenes conflict with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:07.920
executive producer and director Lana Wachowski&nbsp;
— apparently, the two began clashing during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:12.420
table reads for season two in Berlin. Rather&nbsp;
than working out their differences before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:16.500
cameras began rolling, Ameen and Wachowski&nbsp;
continued to butt heads when filming for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:21.000
new episodes began in India. As a result,&nbsp;
Ameen was abruptly replaced by a new actor.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:25.020
It's a mystery as to what actually caused&nbsp;
the tensions between Ameen and Wachowski.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:29.280
Perhaps it was merely creative differences&nbsp;
and disagreements about the storyline for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:33.420
Ameen's character in season two. Maybe Ameen and&nbsp;
Wachowski didn't get along on a personal level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.540
and their strained relationship was&nbsp;
beginning to affect the atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:40.734
on set. Whatever the reason, fans&nbsp;
may never get a straight answer.

00:19:40.734 --> 00:19:46.140
Paul Campbell played Billy Keikeya on the 2004&nbsp;
series "Battlestar Galactica." For two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:51.060
his character served as the personal assistant to&nbsp;
President Laura Roslin. But Campbell did not sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:55.500
an official contract with the show, and while&nbsp;
he was working on "Battlestar Galactica," he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.500 --> 00:20:00.060
was still open to new opportunities. At the&nbsp;
beginning of season two, he landed a role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:04.440
a new pilot which took him away from filming for&nbsp;
a few episodes. When said pilot didn't work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:08.940
he returned to "Battlestar Galactica" — but&nbsp;
his appearances on the show became sporadic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.500
and the showrunners were getting frustrated with&nbsp;
his absences. This resulted in his character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.640
being killed off. Campbell explained in&nbsp;
a 2006 interview with The Scifi World,

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:22.020
"So after that [they] kind of gave me the&nbsp;
ultimatum and said, 'Sign a contract for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.340
five years, or we kind of need to go our separate&nbsp;
ways.' And I kind of put it off and put it off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:30.600
then eventually they just said, 'Look, we can&nbsp;
tell you're not really committed to the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:34.260
and we can't write story lines. So&nbsp;
we've decided to kill the character.'"

